    "created_at": "2016-10-10T21:41:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-10-10T21:42:43Z",
    "body": "> I don't understand what placeholder value means or why browsers do not have something like what I propose today. If you have two processes that want to start a shared worker or write and read a cookie from disk, that needs to happen in an orderly fashion in some kind of manager thread. Otherwise the read can happen while the cookie is half written, or each process gets a unique worker despite it being shareable, etc.\n\nIn gecko we use the main thread for this.  The placeholder is inserted here:\n\nhttps://dxr.mozilla.org/mozilla-central/source/dom/workers/RuntimeService.cpp#1602\n\nAnd then we look for the placeholder here:\n\nhttps://dxr.mozilla.org/mozilla-central/source/dom/workers/RuntimeService.cpp#2437\n\nOf course this does not support cross-process yet.\n\nEdit: Also \"placeholder\" here really means a worker that is in the process of starting up.\n\nIn general, though, I do think it would be nice to have spec language that says something like:\n\n> These steps all happen on the same thread.  It could be the main thread or some other background thread.  But they must operate with event loop semantics with run-to-completion, etc.\n\nHaving names for these threads would make it easier to know which things are expected to maintain ordering with each other, etc.  For example, ServiceWorkerManager could use one named thread for its lifecycle state machine while SharedWorker creation could use a different named thread.  (Today we do both on the main thread in practice, but could move them to other threads.)\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
