    "updated_at": "2017-09-13T12:39:01Z",
    "body": "> The parallel queue you'd just create ahead of time somehow?\r\n\r\nYeah, it'd usually be created at the same time as the thing you want queued access to.\r\n\r\nHere's some prose that I'm currently writing for background fetch:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<div dfn-for=\"service worker registration\">\r\n  A [=service worker registration=] has an associated <dfn>active background fetches</dfn>,\r\n  a [=map=],\r\n  where each key is a DOMString,\r\n  and each item is a [=/background fetch=].\r\n  It is initially an empty [=map=].\r\n\r\n  A [=service worker registration=] has an associated <dfn>active background fetches queue</dfn>,\r\n  a [=queue=],\r\n  initially the result of [=creating a parallel queue=].\r\n</div>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn the above, I'm using the queue to make safe modifications to *active background fetches*.\r\n\r\nIs it worth making an HTML PR for this? The only thing I'm unsure about is returning a queue, rather than some kind of abstraction that you can only enqueue to.",
